Z||000000||Scott2M4||12-21-1999||12:49 PM||||Go to this site, scroll down until you get to the "latest additions" section and click on the three thumbnail photos:<BR> <A HREF="http://home.neo.rr.com/fieros/" TARGET=_blank>http://home.neo.rr.com/fieros/</A> <P>I think the addition of the 16" Z24 rims looks <B>GREAT!</B>  Question is, is this a direct swap?  I didn't see anywhere on the site where they mentioned having to perform modifications.<P>I'd like to put these on my 87SC.||||reg||4
Z||000001||Monkeyman||12-21-1999||01:01 PM||||I don't know anything about the Cavalier rims, but I think you need to check the offset personally before you spend any money.  I've read numerous places that all Beretta rims will fit so I got a set from a '90 Beretta GT and the offset is wrong.  The offset is something like 34mm measured from the center of the rim.||||reg||1
Z||000002||ChadMan||12-21-1999||02:14 PM||||I know that car - the owner's in our local club.  Those wheels do look really nice, but Monkey's right about the offset.  I don't think it's off enough to seriously affect handling, but not much of the wheel protrudes past the bolts, so the wheels don't "flush out" the wheel wells like stock Fiero rims do.<P>But the car's still sweet, with a sharp-looking yellow paint job.  (I'm not flaming you, John...anybody with the patience to build an F40 deserves respect.)||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||12-21-1999||07:52 PM||||Aren't the 14 fiero AL wheels +35mm offset? if bareta is +34 then it's a non issue. 1mm won't matter. could be you measured wrong that much. now if they are -34mm that's a problem.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Monkeyman||12-21-1999||10:18 PM||||theogre--I'm not sure of the numbers on the Beretta wheels, but I tried to put one on the back of my '86 SC and it doesn't seem to fit.  It rubs the (I don't know what it's called) metal part under the spring.  It's the wheel that's rubbing, not the tire.  I'm sure that it's a 7" wide rim (although I haven't actually measured-I suppose I should) so it must be the offset that's off.  I wish it would fit, then I could put them on my new '85 GT when it gets here.  Assuming a negative offset is a "deep dish" RWD rim, then these have a positive offset.  They look the same as the Fiero GT rims and I got them for free, so I hope I can figure something out?||||reg||1
Z||000005||DRH||12-22-1999||03:01 PM||||Monkeyman, Just a thought... If it's close to fitting, you might be able to use a spacer and use those wheels. They fit between the hub and the wheel and basically change the offset of the wheel. Depending on how thick they are you may also need longer studs. <P>I've never used them myself and have heard good and bad stories about thm. There might have even been a discussion here at some point. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||Scott2M4||12-22-1999||03:30 PM||||Someone's selling a pretty nice set of 15" rims on eBay:<P> <A HREF="http://cgi.ebay.com/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=223010783" TARGET=_blank>http://cgi.ebay.com/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=223010783</A> <P>The seller is going by the name <B>Wheelmart</B>.  If you look at their website, they say the rims have a 45mm offset (about 10mm too much from what I understand, right?).  Too bad, $300 ain't bad for a new set of good-looking rims.<P><B>HEY!  Anyone else notice what's at the bottom of the 'Reply to Topic' window now!!!???  Cliff thinks of everything!!!</B><p>[This message has been edited by Scott2M4 (edited 12-22-1999).]||||reg||1
